  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Future Developments</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">For the latest information about developments related to Form 1040-X and its instructions, such as legislation enacted after they were published, go to <a href="#" style="color: blue;">IRS.gov/Form1040X</a>.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">What's New</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">New attachment requirement. For paper-filed Form 1040-X, you must attach to your completed Form 1040-X a completed and updated Form 1040, SR-1040, or SR-1040-NR, with your changes. See <a href="#" style="color: blue;">Assembling Your Return</a>.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Reminders</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">When to use these instructions. These instructions can be used with the December 2025 and later revisions of Form 1040-X, unless later instructions state otherwise.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Form 1040-X and its instructions were converted from an annual revision to continuous use in tax year 2021. The form and instructions are updated as needed to reflect changes in tax law, and the latest version is available at <a href="#" style="color: blue;">IRS.gov/Form1040X</a>. See the instructions for the year you are amending for guidance on specific topics.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Electronic filing available for Form 1040-X. You can file Form 1040-X electronically using the IRS Free File program, or through other IRS-approved software. For more information, see <a href="#" style="color: blue;">IRS.gov/Filing/AmendingReturn</a>.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Use these instructions with both the paper-filed Form 1040-X and the electronically filed Form 1040-X.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">General Instructions</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If you discover an error after filing your return, you may need to amend your return. Use Form 1040-X to correct a previously filed Form 1040, 1040-SR, 1040-NR, or other applicable return. You can also use Form 1040-X to make a claim for a change in tax liability or to request a refund. For more information, see <a href="#" style="color: blue;">IRS.gov/Form1040X</a>.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Many find the easiest way to figure the entries for Form 1040-X is to first review the original return and the instructions for the year the return is being amended.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Purpose of Form</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Form 1040-X is used to make changes to a previously filed tax return. Make certain elections after the prescribed deadline (see instructions for the year you are amending) to ensure your changes are accepted.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">How to use Form 1040-X. You may use Form 1040-X to correct a previously filed Form 1040, 1040-SR, 1040-NR, or other applicable return. You may also use Form 1040-X to claim a refund or to request a change in tax liability. For more information, see <a href="#" style="color: blue;">IRS.gov/Form1040X</a>.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Disclaimer. The IRS does not guarantee that all changes you make to your return will be accepted. The IRS reserves the right to deny any changes you make to your return.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Which Revision To Use</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Use the revision of Form 1040-X that corresponds to the tax year you are amending. If you are amending a return from a year before the current revision, use the revision that was in effect for that tax year.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Interest and Penalties</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Interest and penalties may be assessed on the amount of tax you owe due to the amendment. You may need to pay interest and penalties when you file Form 1040-X.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">When To File</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">File Form 1040-X only after you have filed your original return. You may amend your original return by filing Form 1040-X more than once, as long as each amendment is filed within the time limit for the original return or within 3 years after the date you paid the tax, whichever is later. If you file your original return early, you may file Form 1040-X later. If you file your original return late, you may file Form 1040-X later, but only within the time limit for the original return.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Special Situations</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Foreign tax credit. If you are claiming a foreign tax credit, you must file Form 1116. You must also attach a statement to Form 1040-X showing the amount of foreign tax credit you are claiming. See Form 1116 instructions for more information.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Loss or credit carryback. If you are claiming a loss or a credit carryback, you must attach a statement to Form 1040-X showing the amount of the loss or credit and the tax year to which it applies. See the instructions for the relevant form for more information.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Partnership income. If you are amending a return that includes income from a partnership, you must attach a statement to Form 1040-X showing the partnership's name, your partnership interest, and the amount of income or loss you are claiming. See the instructions for the relevant form for more information.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Other penalties. Other penalties can be imposed for substantial understatement of tax, failure to file, or failure to pay. See Pub. 517, Your Federal Income Tax, for more information.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Tracking Your Amended Return</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">You must track your amended return to ensure you receive any refund due. Keep a copy of Form 1040-X and all attachments for at least three years.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Specific Instructions</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Calendar or Fiscal Year. Determine the calendar year or fiscal year of the return you are amending.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Name, Current Address, and Social Security Number (SSN). If you and your spouse are amending a joint return, list your names and SSNs in the same order as on the original return. If you are amending a single return, list your name and SSN. If you are amending a return for a dependent, list the dependent's name and SSN.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Change of address. If you have moved since you filed your original return, enter your current address on Form 1040-X. If you are amending a return for a dependent, enter the dependent's current address.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Foreign address. If you have a foreign address, enter the city, state, and country of the foreign address. If you have a foreign address on that line, but also complete the spaces below that line, don't enter the country name. Follow the country's practice for postal codes and the name of the province, county, or state.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">ITINs for all. If you don't have an SSN, but you already have an ITIN, enter it if it is the same as the SSN on the original return. If you have a new ITIN, enter it. If you have no SSN or ITIN, leave the space blank.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Presidential Election Campaign. You can use Form 1040-X to designate $3 to the Presidential Election Campaign Fund if you or your spouse if filing jointly. If you are filing separately, you can designate $3 to the Presidential Election Campaign Fund for yourself or your spouse.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Amended Return Filing Status. Check the box that corresponds to your filing status on this return. If you are changing your filing status, you must attach a statement explaining the change. If you are changing from a single to a joint return, you must attach a statement explaining the change. If you are changing from a joint to a single return, you must attach a statement explaining the change.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Line 1 - Adjusted Gross Income</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Enter your adjusted gross income (AGI), which is the total of your income minus deductions (adjustments). Any change to the amount of your AGI must be shown on Form 1040-X.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Line 2 - Itemized Deductions or Standard Deduction</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If you itemize your deductions, enter the total amount of your itemized deductions. If you take the standard deduction, enter the amount of the standard deduction for your filing status. If you are changing from itemizing to taking the standard deduction, or vice versa, you must attach a statement explaining the change.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Line 4a - Deductions for Tips, Overtime, Car Loan Interest, and Seniors From Schedule 1 (Form 1040)</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If claiming any of these deductions, attach your Schedule 1-A (Form 1040) and these deductions were introduced in 2025.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Line 5 - Taxable Income</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If the taxable income on the return you are amending is $0 and you have made changes on Form 1040-X, you must enter $0 on Line 5. If you have made changes that result in taxable income, enter the amount of the taxable income on the return you are amending.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Tax Liability</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Include on line 6 the amount you reported on Schedule 2 (Form 1040), line 1, for the year you are amending.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Line 6 - Tax</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Figure the tax on your taxable income shown on line 5, using the tax tables or tax rate schedules for the year you are amending. If you are changing your tax liability, you must attach a statement explaining the change.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Line 7 - Nonrefundable Credits</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Enter your total nonrefundable credits in column A. Nonrefundable credits are those that reduce your tax, but any excess isn't refunded to you.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Line 10 - Other Taxes</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Enter the amount of any other taxes you are claiming as a credit on your amended return.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Payments</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Enter the total amount of payments you made on your original return and on your amended return.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Line 12 - Withholding</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Enter the total amount of income tax withheld from your wages, interest, dividends, and other sources on your original return and on your amended return.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Line 13 - Estimated Tax Payments</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Enter the total amount of estimated tax payments you made on your original return and on your amended return.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Line 14 - Earned Income Credit (EIC)</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Enter the amount of the Earned Income Credit you are claiming on your amended return.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Line 15 - Refundable Credits</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Enter the total amount of refundable credits you are claiming on your amended return.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Line 16 - Amount Paid With Extension or Tax Return</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Enter the total amount of tax you paid with an extension or on your original return.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Line 17 - Total Payments</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Enter the total of lines 12, 13, 14, 15, and 16.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Refund or Amount You Owe</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Enter the difference between your total payments (Line 17) and your total tax liability (Line 6). If the result is positive, you are due a refund. If the result is negative, you owe tax.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Line 18 - Overpayment</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Enter the amount of overpayment you are claiming on your amended return.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Line 19 - Amount Available To Pay Additional Tax</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Enter the amount of additional tax you are willing to pay on your amended return.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Line 20 - Amount You Owe</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Enter the final amount of tax you owe after considering all credits and payments.</p>
  </div>
</div>

