<div class="page">
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start;">
    <div>
      <h2 style="font-size: 16pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">Instructions for Form 1040-X (Rev. December 2025)</h2>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If you made any changes to Form 1040-X, lines 1 through 6, be sure to figure out your original credits. Attach the appropriate schedule(s) to your return.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Credit for qualifying children and other dependents. If you are amending a return with a retroactive claim of the child tax credit, you must have an SSN valid for employment issued for the child before the due date for filing the return you are amending, excluding extensions. If you are amending a return with a retroactive claim of the credit for other dependents, the dependent you claim must have been issued an SSN, ITIN, or adoption taxpayer identification number (ATIN) on or before the due date for filing the return you are amending, excluding extensions. Your child tax credit may be refundable. For additional information, see Schedule 8812 (Form 1040) and its instructions for the year of the tax return you are amending.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="text-align: right;">
      <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">SSN required.</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If you didn't have an SSN on or before the due date of your return for the tax year being amended (including extensions), you can't claim the EIC on your amended return. If you are amending a return with a retroactive claim of the credit for other dependents, you can't count that child as a qualifying child in figuring the amount of the EIC on your amended return.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Line 9—Requested for Future Use</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">This line is reserved for future use. Do not enter an amount on this line.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Line 10—Other Taxes</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Enter in column A the amount you reported on the line for total other taxes from Schedule 2 (Form 1040) for the year you are amending.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Payments</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If you made any changes to Form 1040-X, lines 1 through 6, you may need to figure other taxes that were included in the same section on your original return.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Line 12—Withholding</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">In column A, enter from the return you are amending any federal income tax withheld and any excess social security and tier 1 RTA tax withheld (SS/W/RTA).</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Line 16—Amount Paid With Extension or Tax Return</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">On this line, enter the total of the following amounts:</p>
    <ul style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 5px;">
      <li>Any amount paid with your request for an extension on Form 4868 (Application for Automatic Extension of Time to File U.S. Individual Income Tax Return, or Form 2550, Application for Automatic Extension of Time to Pay U.S. Income Tax Return). Also include any amount paid electronically in connection with an extension of time to file, but don't include the convenience fee you were charged.</li>
      <li>The amount you paid with your original return, regardless of method. Also include any additional payments you made after it was filed, but don't include payments of interest or penalties, or the convenience fee you were charged.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Line 13—Estimated Tax Payments</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">In column A, enter the estimated tax payments you claimed on your original return. If you filed Form 1040-C, U.S. Departing Alien Income Tax Return, include on this line the amount paid as the balance due with that return. Also include any of your prior year's overpayment that you elected to apply to estimated tax payments for the year you are amending.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Line 14—Earned Income Credit (EIC)</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Include any amounts from Form 8689, Allocation of Individual Income Tax to the U.S. Virgin Islands, that are being included in the total payments line on your Form 1040 or 1040-SR. For additional information, see Form 8689 for the year of the tax return you are amending.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Line 17—Total Payments</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If you are amending your return to claim or change a refundable credit, attach the appropriate schedule(s) or form(s).</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Line 22—Overpayment Received as Refund</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">The refund amount on line 22 will be sent separately from any refund you claimed on your original return (see Line 18). We will figure any interest and include it in your refund.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Line 18—Overpayment</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Enter the overpayment from your original return. If your original return was changed by the IRS and the result was an additional overpayment of tax, also include that amount on line 18. Don't include interest you received on any refund. Any additional refund you are entitled to on Form 1040-X will be sent separately from any refund you haven't yet received from your original return.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Line 19—Amount Available To Pay Additional Tax</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If line 18 is larger than line 17, line 19 will be negative. You will owe additional tax. To figure the amount owed, treat the amount on line 19 as positive and add it to the amount on line 11. Enter the result on line 20.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Line 20—Amount You Owe</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">The IRS recommends paying the amount due on line 20 electronically whenever possible. If you file Form 1040-X electronically, you can simultaneously authorize the Treasury to make an electronic withdrawal (direct debit) for the balance due.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Line 23—Overpayment Applied to Estimated Tax</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If you owe past-due federal or state debts, all or part of your overpayment will be used to pay the past-due amounts. You will receive a notice if any of your overpayment has been applied to past-due amounts, so you will know how much was applied to past-due amounts.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Part I—Dependents</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">These lines are reserved for future use. Do not enter an amount on these lines.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Line 30—Dependents</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">List all dependents claimed on this amended return. This</p>
    <ul style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 5px;">
      <li>Dependents claimed on your original return who are still being claimed on this return, and</li>
      <li>Dependents not claimed on your original return who are being added to this return.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Part II—Explanation of Changes</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">The IRS needs to know why you are filing Form 1040-X. For</p>
    <ul style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 5px;">
      <li>Received another Form W-2 after your filed return,</li>
      <li>Forgot to claim the child tax credit or the credit for other dependents, or</li>
      <li>Made a mistake on your original return.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Part III—Direct Deposit (For Filed Form 1040-X Only)</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Form 8397 may be filed electronically by attaching it to an electronically filed Form 1040-X even if the filer is not amending their tax return.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Sign Your Return</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Form 1040-X is a valid return unless you sign it in accordance with the requirements in these instructions. If you are amending a return, you must sign it. If you are filing a new return, you must sign it.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If you are amending a return, you are still responsible for the accuracy of the information you provide. If you are filing a new return, you are responsible for the accuracy of the information you provide.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If you are filing a new return, you must sign it. If you are amending a return, you must sign it.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Identity Protection Personal Identification Number (PIN)</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If you received an PIN from the IRS, enter it in the PIN space on Form 1040-X. If you don't receive an PIN, you can use a valid signature.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If you receive an PIN, you must enter it. If you don't receive an PIN, you must sign your return.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If you are filing a new return, you must sign it. If you are amending a return, you must sign it.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">All taxpayers are now eligible for an PIN. For more information, see Pub. 5477. To apply for an PIN, go to IRS.gov/PIN and use the Get an PIN tool.</h3>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Requirements for a Paper Return</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">You must have your signature on your return if you file it on paper. Digital, electronic, or typed signatures are not valid signatures for Forms 1040-X.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Requirements for an Electronic Return</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">To file your return electronically, you must sign the return electronically using a personal identification number (PIN) and provide the information described below. If you are filing online, you must use a PIN. If you are filing through a tax professional, you can use a Self-Select PIN or a Practitioner PIN. If you use an PIN, you must enter it in the PIN space on Form 1040-X. If you are filing through a tax professional, they must use a Practitioner PIN.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">Where To File</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">You can file Form 1040-X electronically with tax filing software to avoid mailing it to the IRS. You can also mail it to the IRS.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">If you are amending your return on paper because of any of the situations listed next, use the corresponding address.</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">If you are filing</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">in response to a notice you received from the IRS</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">with Form 1040-NR or 1040-SR</p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt;">If none of the situations listed above apply to you, mail your amended return to the Internal Revenue Service Center shown next that applies to you.</h3>
  </div>
</div>

